# gotp Roadmap


## Tests

No tests for process, gen_server, or supervisor. Only assert/ and process/ctrie_test.go exist.
Need test suites for:
- process/ — spawn lifecycle, link/monitor propagation, exit handling, message delivery
- gen_server/ — call/cast/continue/handleinfo flow, timeout, terminate
- supervisor/static/ — restart strategies, restart intensity, child ordering, shutdown, auto-shutdown
- Each new module below gets tests as part of implementation


## The Rest of StaticSupervisor

Files: supervisor/static/server.go, supervisor/behaviour.go, supervisor/child_spec.go

TRANSIENT restart is broken. shouldRestart (server.go:312) groups TRANSIENT with TEMPORARY
and both return false. TRANSIENT should restart on abnormal exit (non-normal, non-shutdown).
Only TEMPORARY never restarts.

Restart intensity is per-child right now. Erlang does it per-supervisor with a sliding window:
maintain a []time.Time on the server, one entry per restart. On each restart prepend time.Now(),
prune entries older than ResetPeriod. If len(restarts) > MaxRestarts the supervisor itself
terminates. This catches cascading failures — 4 different children crashing in quick succession
should kill the supervisor, per-child tracking misses that completely.
Erlang optimization: keep an nrestarts counter alongside the list. When nrestarts < intensity
skip the traversal (O(1) fast path). Only traverse when counter hits the threshold.

Shutdown sends KILL immediately (server.go:203). Should send shutdown first, wait up to the
child's Shutdown timeout, then escalate to KILL if the child hasn't exited. The ReceiveWithTimeout
already handles the wait.

Child ordering (the TODO at server.go:217-222): currently sorts by PID descending as proxy for
reverse-start-order. Breaks when children restart (new PID != original position). Erlang uses
{[child_id()], #{child_id() => child_rec()}} — ordered list + map. Replace children/childIDs
maps with:
- order []gotp.Atom — child IDs in start order
- byID map[gotp.Atom]child — O(1) lookup by ID
- byPID map[PID]gotp.Atom — reverse PID → ID for ExitMsg handling
Eliminates sort, fixes rest_for_one and one_for_all ordering.


## The Rest of DynamicSupervisor

Erlang's simple_one_for_one extracted into its own module by Elixir. Starts with zero children,
all added via start_child. Only ONE_FOR_ONE (no ordering dependencies between children).

Erlang internals: children stored in map[PID]child, no ordering, O(1) insert/delete.
Temporary children don't store start args (mapsets vs maps internally) — saves memory.
Shutdown is parallel: signal all children, collect DOWN messages with shared timer.
Child spec ID is ignored — identified by PID only.
dynamic_restarts counter tracks in-flight restart attempts for failed restarts.

Erlang uses a single template spec, start_child appends extra args.
Elixir DynamicSupervisor accepts per-child specs, no template.

max_children: hard cap on concurrent children (default unlimited), returns error when exceeded.
extra_arguments: Elixir prepends shared args to every child's start call.

start_child blocks the supervisor process. This is the throughput ceiling. PartitionSupervisor
exists to solve this. terminate_child also blocks. which_children is O(n) and can OOM with
large child populations.

Restart intensity is per-supervisor (same sliding window). If any child causes intensity to be
exceeded the entire supervisor terminates.

Parallel shutdown:
1. Monitor all children and send exit signals to all of them
2. Start single shared timer (Shutdown timeout)
3. Wait for all DOWN messages
4. Timer fires before all down? kill remaining with exit(P, kill)
5. Collect errors after all children are down


## PartitionSupervisor

Wraps N instances of any supervisor type, routes by caller PID hash. Default N = GOMAXPROCS.
Exists because DynamicSupervisor and Task.Supervisor are single-process bottlenecks.
start_child/terminate_child serialize through one mailbox. PartitionSupervisor shards children
across N independent supervisor instances.

Each partition is a full supervisor with its own restart intensity tracking.
API surface identical to the wrapped supervisor — transparent to callers.


## The Rest of Registry

Local, decentralized, scalable key-value process storage. Not distributed. Automatically cleans
up entries when registered processes die. Separate from the global process.nameTree — this is
user-space.

Two key modes:
- Unique — each key maps to 0 or 1 process. Lookup hits one partition.
- Duplicate — key maps to multiple processes. Lookup searches all partitions.
  - partition by key (many topics, few subscribers)
  - partition by PID (few topics, many subscribers)

Core API: register(key, value), lookup(key), dispatch(key, callback), match/select with
patterns, update_value (unique only).

Via-tuple integration: GenServer/Supervisor naming through the registry.
Listeners: notification on register/unregister events.
Meta: arbitrary metadata on the registry.

Partitioning: default 1 partition. For high concurrency set to schedulers_online() equivalent.

Cleanup: monitor registered processes. Process dies, entries removed. Slightly async (brief
window where lookups may return stale entries).

Go considerations: CTriE could serve unique keys. Duplicate needs key → []Ref. Sharded
sync.RWMutex + map may be simpler than ETS emulation. Weak references give automatic cleanup
but non-deterministic timing. Monitor-based (ExitMsg) gives deterministic removal.


## Task

Process for executing a single action. Structured async/await, supervision integration, caller
tracking. Tasks must be supervised through the init system.

The Task handle:
- pid — task process
- ref — monitor reference (message key for result delivery)
- owner — process that created the task

async/await (linked): Task.async spawns both linked and monitored. Task sends result as
{ref, result}. Task.await blocks for result (default 5s). Either crashes, both crash.
Must await or yield — reply message is always sent, ignoring it orphans messages in the
caller's mailbox.

async_nolink (monitored only): same but only monitored. Caller survives task failure. Requires
TEMPORARY restart because restarting gives new PID which invalidates the caller's reference.
Success: {ref, result}. Failure: DOWN message.

yield: non-blocking check for result. Can call repeatedly.
Pattern: yield(task, timeout) || shutdown(task)

Fire-and-forget: start/start_link. No result, just side effects. Not linked to caller,
linked to supervisor only.

Streaming: async_stream — process enumerable concurrently. max_concurrency defaults to
schedulers_online(). ordered: true buffers to maintain input order. Greedy about concurrency.

Caller tracking: $callers chain of PIDs that initiated the task. Distinct from $ancestors
(supervision hierarchy). Crash logs include callers.

Don't await inside GenServer — blocks the server. Use async_nolink + handle result in HandleInfo.

Task.Supervisor: DynamicSupervisor specialized for tasks. Wraps dynamic supervisor, creates
task-specific child specs (TEMPORARY restart, 5s shutdown). Provides async, async_nolink,
start_child as first-class operations.


## PubSub

Nice to have. Built on duplicate-mode Registry + dispatch. Processes register as subscribers
under a topic key, dispatch sends messages to all registered PIDs for that key. Warrants its
own module for ergonomic API even though it's a Registry use case.


## Run as VM / Builder / Both

Run as VM - load the .so file and run the Application interface
Run as Builder - build the .so file and run the Application interface
Run as Both - one single command to run the Application interface either to handle building
before running when needed

Go plugin package provides .so loading on Linux. Plugin exports a symbol implementing
application.Application. Runtime loads, resolves symbol, passes to grts.Boot().

Enables hot-code-loading-adjacent workflows: rebuild plugin, restart VM, new code runs.
Not true hot code replacement (Erlang code_change) but a practical equivalent for development.


## Application Config with Overlays

Layered config for Applications. Base in code, overlaid by environment-specific (dev/staging/prod),
overlaid by runtime (CLI flags, env vars).

Config available before Application.Start is called. Overlay order:
code defaults → config file → env vars → CLI flags

May need additional Application callback (Configure?) or pass through StartType.
Erlang equivalent: application:get_env/set_env for runtime config access.


## Node

Existing code: internal/node/

What exists: node identity (atom@host with ParseName), local server (singleton, manages
connections), remote client (outbound), 3-way HMAC nonce handshake, TLS connections via
net/rpc, connection management (known/visible/hidden maps).

What's missing:
- Process-level distributed send: process.Send(remote_pid, msg) routing through node connections
- Distributed monitor/link
- Net kernel equivalent: auto-reconnection, node up/down notifications
- Init system integration: node server should be supervised in the boot tree
- The RPC goroutines have a TODO for linked process spawns


## Message Serialization

Existing: term/term.go using encoding/gob with buffer pooling.

Current direction: gob + Go net/rpc for inter-node transport. Native encoding, no external
dependencies, handles arbitrary types through registration. No protobuf schema requirement —
that's not user friendly as a hard requirement for the term system.

If serialization becomes a bottleneck later: gob optimizations (pre-registered types, buffer
reuse — some already done), Cap'n Proto/FlatBuffers for zero-copy, or custom binary encoding
for hot-path message types.


## Clustering

Existing code: internal/gpmd_2/ (GPMD v2 — RPC on port 4469 with TLS), internal/gpmd/ (GPMD v1
— JSON over TCP), internal/node/, cmd/gpmd/ (CLI tool).

GPMD v2 supports Register/Unregister/Heartbeat/List/GetByName/GetByHost.
GPMD v1 uses JSON encoding with action-based protocol.
CLI: daemon mode, name listing, node registration with heartbeat (60s interval).

What's needed:
- Distributed process send: route process.Send(remote_pid, msg) through node connections
- Distributed spawn on remote node
- Global name registration across all connected nodes
- Node monitoring: notifications when nodes go up or down
- Net split handling: partition and rejoin behavior
- Heartbeat-based liveness: GPMD has it, node server should use it to detect dead connections
